--TEST--
Core\Math::toBase writes a negative number with a leading sign where dechex writes its two's complement and base_convert drops the sign, and ::fromBase throws on a digit the base cannot hold, on no digits at all and on an answer past int, where hexdec and bindec each repair it into a number
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Four divergences, and all four are the same decision taken at four sites: PHP's base conversions have no failure mode at all, so every input they cannot read is turned into *some* number, and `Core\Math::toBase`/`::fromBase` refuse instead. **A negative number.** `Core\Math::toBase(-255, 16)` is `-ff` — the sign written as a character and the magnitude taken through `i128`, so `INT_MIN` is `-8000000000000000` and needs no special case. PHP's three dedicated spellings reinterpret the `int` as an unsigned 64-bit word, so `dechex(-255)` is `ffffffffffffff01` and `decbin(-1)` is sixty-four `1`s; `base_convert("-255", 10, 16)` takes the third answer, dropping the `-` as a character it has no digit for — with an `E_DEPRECATED` since PHP 7.4 — and giving `ff`. Three answers for one input is the reason MWL has a fourth: a sign is not a digit, so it is carried rather than reinterpreted or discarded, and the round trip through `fromBase` therefore holds for a negative exactly as it does for a positive, which is what the counted sweep at the foot asserts and is true of none of PHP's three. **A digit the base cannot hold.** `hexdec("beefy")` is `48879` — the `y` is dropped and the remaining digits are read as though the caller had written them — which is the quiet wrong answer ADR 0063 R4 makes a throw. **No digits at all.** `bindec("")` and `octdec("")` are both `0`, so the absence of a number and the number zero are the same value; `fromBase` refuses an empty string, and refuses a lone `-` for the same reason. **An answer past `int`.** `hexdec("ffffffffffffffff")` widens silently to the `float` `1.8446744073709552E+19`, losing every low bit on the way; ADR 0007 § 4 makes an overflow a throw rather than a change of type, so `fromBase` refuses it, and refuses `"9223372036854775808"` in base 10 for the same reason where `base_convert` hands the spelling straight back. The base bound itself is *not* a divergence — both languages refuse a base outside 2 to 36 — and every agreeing row is in `math-to-base-and-from-base-match-decbin-dechex-decoct-and-base_convert`.
--FILE--
<?mwl
// Spec § 3's `toBase` and `fromBase` rows, on the inputs where the two
// members and their PHP twins part company. The agreeing half is in
// `math-to-base-and-from-base-match-decbin-dechex-decoct-and-base_convert`.

// A negative number is written with a leading `-` and its own magnitude's
// digits. `dechex(-255)` is `ffffffffffffff01` — the two's complement of the
// 64-bit word — and `base_convert("-255", 10, 16)` is `ff`, having dropped
// the sign as a character base 16 has no digit for.
echo Core\Math::toBase(-255, 16), " ", Core\Math::toBase(-5, 2), " ", Core\Math::toBase(-35, 36), "\n";

// The widest negative there is, which is the one value a magnitude-and-sign
// spelling has to take through a wider integer to reach at all. PHP writes
// `8000000000000000` here, indistinguishable from the unsigned value.
echo Core\Math::toBase(Core\Math::INT_MIN, 16), "\n";

// The sign is one character, not a change of width: a spelling is as long as
// its own magnitude needs plus one. PHP's is as long as the word — `decbin(-1)`
// is sixty-four characters and `dechex(-255)` sixteen.
echo Core\Str::length(Core\Math::toBase(-1, 2)), " ", Core\Str::length(Core\Math::toBase(-255, 16)), "\n";

// And it reads back. `hexdec("-ff")` is `255` — the sign dropped again, so
// PHP's read of a negative spelling is the positive number.
echo Core\Math::fromBase("-ff", 16), " ", Core\Math::fromBase("-101", 2), "\n";

// A digit the base has no room for. `hexdec("beefy")` answers `48879`,
// reading the digits it recognized and ignoring the one it did not; the same
// happens to a `2` in base 2 and to every letter above the base's own last.
try {
    var $trailing = Core\Math::fromBase("beefy", 16);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $pastAlphabet) {
    echo "refused\n";
}
try {
    var $binary = Core\Math::fromBase("102", 2);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $notABit) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// No digits at all. `bindec("")` is `0`, which makes the absence of a number
// and the number zero one value; a lone sign is the same case with the sign
// stripped first.
try {
    var $nothing = Core\Math::fromBase("", 2);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $noDigits) {
    echo "refused\n";
}
try {
    var $signOnly = Core\Math::fromBase("-", 16);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $signAlone) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// An answer past `int`. `hexdec("ffffffffffffffff")` widens to a `float` and
// keeps none of the low bits; `base_convert("9223372036854775808", 10, 10)`
// hands the spelling back unchanged, one past `INT_MAX`.
try {
    var $unsigned = Core\Math::fromBase("ffffffffffffffff", 16);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $pastUnsigned) {
    echo "refused\n";
}
try {
    var $oneOver = Core\Math::fromBase("9223372036854775808", 10);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $pastMax) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// `INT_MAX` itself is the last accepted answer, named beside the first
// refused one above, and `INT_MIN` is the other end — a magnitude one larger
// than `INT_MAX`'s, which is why it is reached through the sign rather than
// through the digits.
echo Core\Math::fromBase("7fffffffffffffff", 16), " ", Core\Math::fromBase("-8000000000000000", 16), "\n";

// The round trip through a negative, counted. It holds for every row here and
// for none of PHP's three spellings: `dechex` round-trips through `hexdec`
// into a `float` past `INT_MAX`, and `base_convert` loses the sign on the way
// out, so both answer a positive number for a negative input.
array<int> $negatives = [-1, -255, -4095, -1000000, Core\Math::INT_MIN];
int $held = 0;
int $rows = 0;
foreach ($negatives as int $n) {
    $rows = $rows + 1;
    if (Core\Math::fromBase(Core\Math::toBase($n, 36), 36) == $n) {
        $held = $held + 1;
    }
}
echo $held, " ", $rows, "\n";
--EXPECT--
-ff -101 -z
-8000000000000000
2 3
-255 -5
refused
refused
refused
refused
refused
refused
9223372036854775807 -9223372036854775808
5 5
